a bipartisan group of senators is  pushing for reforms to combat sexual  misconduct in the military and in  Congress this morning New York  Democratic senator Kirsten Gillibrand is  reintroducing the military justice  Improvement Act it seeks to  professionalize how the military  prosecutes sexual assault  senator Gillibrand yesterday introduced  the me to Congress act she says too many  people are not taking the problem of  sexual harassment in Congress seriously  Gillibrand joins us from Capitol Hill  center - good morning good morning how  pervasive is the problem on Capitol Hill  and what do you think can be done to  stop it  I think the problem is pervasive and  what we have right now is the process  that's opaque difficult to navigate and  most survivors don't even know where to  report so our bill is going to change  all that make it transparent make it  more accountable and will actually have  a survey so every person who works here  can fill out a Climate Survey about  whether they feel safe whether they've  been harassed if so did they report if  not why not  Senate Speaker Ryan will introduce a  bill in the house will they be different  bills I don't know what his bill will  say but it's more than just having  training he said he wants to do training  that's not enough we need training 1 but  we also need to change the process so  that it's possible for someone to get  justice today if you have someone  harassing you at your office you'll have  to go through a mediation process for  months before you can even report your  case that is not something that's  conducive to people reporting or ending  harassment in the workplace  congresswoman Speier testified the other  day that there are two current members  of Congress one Democrat and Republican  who have engaged in this kind of  behavior but she did not name them do  you think they should be named well  that's up to the survivors a survivor  might not want to have to be in the  middle of a story for the next X number  of months she might want to continue  with her career she may might not want  to be discriminated against or somehow  retaliated against so the way I've  written my bill is to make sure the  survivor has the choice about whether or  not she wants to take the issue public  or he because right now they don't even  have that option they have to fight they  have to actually sign an agreement to  not disclose what happened to them Mitch  McConnell and others have floated the  idea of having Jeff Sessions serve as a  write-in candidate  his old seat in the Senate let me ask  you whether or not you would agree with  that and would you support that and if  it were between Jeff Sessions and Wymore  who would you rather serve within the  Senate well there's a great candidate  who's running on the Democratic line who  I support but from a Republican  standpoint because obviously we know  that the Republican is the front-runner  there if I had to choose a Republican  right and I would pick Luther strange so  you are not in favor of Jeff Sessions  serving as a write-in I just gave you my  answer if Senator Moore is elected do  you think he should be seated no I would  vote against him being seated actually  all right senator can you talk about  your military justice Improvement Act  how will it reduce or reduce or  eliminate sexual assaults how would it  work exactly  so the problem today is that every  Secretary of Defense for the last 20  years has said zero tolerance for sexual  assault the military but in fact last  year there over 15,000 cases estimated  and so what we have is a huge problem  where survivors don't necessarily feel  comfortable coming forward because last  year 59 percent who did reported that  they were retaliated against for doing  so and so what we have to do is change  the dynamic  we don't want commanders making the  decisions about which cases have to go  forward to trial in which cases don't  because those commanders are neither  lawyers nor prosecutors nor trained and  may well have biases so we need  transparency and accountability and so  the way the bill is written is we want  the decision to be made by trained  military prosecutors who have no bias  and who can make the decision based on  what does the evidence say and what we  have right now is a system where very  few cases are going forward to trial  even fewer ending in conviction the  conviction rate and the prosecution rate  have gone down in the last year it's a  problem so we need a military justice  system that is worthy of the sacrifice  that these men and women are making  every day for our country so senator are  you and Senator Claire McCaskill on the  same page on this senator McCaskill and  I have put forward  dozens of reforms that have already  passed into law but all of them have  really not dented the problem they  really are incremental reforms efforts  that haven't made a difference in how  many people are willing to come forward  and how many people are willing  to have their cases be prosecuted and  then how many cases will be prosecuted  and so what we need to do is have a  system that is more professional one  where people can have faith in the  system today they have less faith in the  system the percentage of people who are  reporting openly has gone down  we need that to go up and we need the  number of cases actually going to trial  and ending in conviction to go up we're  going in the wrong direction all right  senator Gillibrand always good to see  you thank you so much for taking the  time this morning
 

